      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-10-04T12:06:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "why not use dynamic profiles for remote submix?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 114,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 118,
        "endChar": 70
      },
      "revId": "30b0b40b83af989f8c9801b650e1354ccd712fb7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "141ad3a5_4e8b0c90",
        "filename": "audio/aidl/default/AudioPolicyConfigXmlConverter.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 118,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042638
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-10-06T19:50:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "With aosp/2775704, we now have two options for dynamic profiles:\n\n1. Both template device port and the mix port have dynamic profiles. In this case, the HAL module must populate profiles of the device port by querying the device, and these get propagated to both ports.\n\n2. The template device port has dynamic profiles, while routable mix ports have static profiles. In this case, the profiles of the connected device can remain blank. This is what we use for remote submix, and this is similar to the configuration we specified before in the XML file.\n\nThus, in any of those cases, the mix port will get concrete profiles at some point. This is important, because when the framework opens a stream with an unspecified configuration, the HAL module can use the profiles of the mix port to pick one. If there are no profiles specified, the HAL module has to hardcode some choice. I don\u0027t think that this is a good approach.",
      "parentUuid": "4ed4bd42_6ead79cc",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 114,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 118,
        "endChar": 70
      },
      "revId": "30b0b40b83af989f8c9801b650e1354ccd712fb7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
